  "notes": "⛔ `low` because the research marks this entry medium-low: a clear stylistic profile with limited technical sourcing [LOW-CONF]. Treat the numbers as a reading of the records. Two research words decide the file. \"Behind-the-beat\": authored as `snare.offGridMs` [3, 8] against the trap parent's 0, plus a loosened `quantizeStrength` of 0.82 — this is the only entry in the batch that moves the backbeat off the grid, and it is what \"laid-back\" means to a MIDI generator. \"Jazz-leaning colour (m7, m9)\": authored as dorian-first scales, `extensions` at 0.55 seventh and 0.25 ninth, and the only ii–v–i family in this batch, over a `vibraphone` timbre for the jazzy guitar / electric-piano / vibraphone loops. The kit is \"intentionally light\", so `velocityTiers` is the lowest here, and the low vocal-melody content is a modest `densityPerBar` in a low register. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the drum numbers and the 808's glide rate — the research says the 808 is \"not the record's focal point\" and gives no figures. ⚠ The ii–v–i family is a reading of \"jazz-leaning colour\", not a progression the research names; it carries the lowest weight and is the first thing to delete if this sounds more harmonically mobile than the records. ⚠ `anticipationProb` raised to 0.15. With `mirror_kick` against a fully locked 808 both parts take every kick, so every bass onset lands exactly on an 808 onset — `coherence.rs` calls that one line played twice rather than two parts locking. The lock is the style and is untouched; the anticipation is what makes them two parts.",
